  'Three U.S. Rebounds Left Almost the Same Hole',
  'The Nasdaq rose nearly twice as much as the Russell 2000 on Monday. Both still ended about 4% below their November 13 closes, alongside the S&P 500.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The biggest bounce left nearly the same hole.</strong><span> The Nasdaq, S&amp;P 500 and Russell 2000 ended Monday 4.16%, 4.29% and 4.33% below their November 13 closes.</span></li>
<li><strong>Monday’s gains were much farther apart.</strong><span> The Nasdaq gained 6.51%, the S&amp;P 500 rose 4.73% and the Russell 2000 advanced 3.40%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Nasdaq had the deepest selloff to recover from.</strong><span> It retrieved 58% of the index points it lost last week. The Russell recovered 42%.</span></li>
<li><strong>Nvidia restored its CEO while its board investigation continued.</strong><span> The stock-market response repriced leadership risk across AI-linked holdings. It leaves project delivery and cash generation to be tested.</span></li>
<li><strong>Use both starting dates on the same portfolio.</strong><span> Measure Friday-to-Monday participation and the drawdown since November 13, then attribute the Canadian-dollar translation separately.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>At Monday’s close, the Nasdaq Composite looked like the clear winner. It advanced 6.51% after Nvidia announced that Jensen Huang was back as chief executive. The S&amp;P 500 gained 4.73% and the small-cap Russell 2000 rose 3.40%. Put all three on the same starting line before last week’s selloff, though, and the finish is remarkably tight. Each was still a little more than 4% below its November 13 close. The Nasdaq’s lead over the Russell on Monday was 3.10 percentage points. Across the full episode it was only 0.17 points.</p>
<h2>The Biggest Bounce Had the Most Ground to Cover</h2>
<p>The Nasdaq lost 10.02% last week, compared with 8.61% for the S&amp;P 500 and 7.48% for the Russell. An index that falls 10% needs a gain of about 11.1% just to recover. Monday’s rebound returned 58% of the Nasdaq’s lost index points. Small caps recovered 42%, having given up fewer points relative to their starting level. The S&amp;P 500 recovered 50%. The three distinct paths left a hypothetical US$100 in each benchmark worth between US$95.67 and US$95.84 before dividends, fees and currency conversion.</p>
