<p style="font-size:11px;color:#56585B;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each of the 17 market closes is shown relative to its benchmark’s November 13 close. The three U.S.-dollar price indexes finished Monday at 95.84, 95.71 and 95.67, respectively. The shaded window covers the five sessions of last week’s selloff.</p>
<p>Overnight futures had already pointed toward a rebound. The afternoon evidence showed how far the recovery spread and where it was strongest. By 3:50 p.m. ET, Investor’s Business Daily reported technology and communication services leading an advance that reached nearly all S&amp;P 500 sectors. Energy was down. The Russell’s closing gain confirms participation well outside the largest tech stocks. Nvidia’s return might have accelerated the shift in sentiment, and recovering buyers had other reasons to come back. The day’s moves cannot isolate the contribution of the board decision.</p>
<h2>Repricing a CEO Takes Hours. Funding the Buildout Takes Years.</h2>
<p>Nvidia said its independent committee’s investigation would continue after Huang returned. A restored chief executive can reduce uncertainty about who will run the company and how it will work with customers. A jump in the shares can also lift a capitalization-weighted benchmark mechanically and prompt investors to reprice suppliers whose prospects depend on the same investments. Neither channel measures how much cash will flow from the projects on which those valuations depend. Oracle’s Friday statement gives investors a more specific question to test. The company said it had secured the funding required for its OpenAI contract, including vendor financing. Delivery, utilization and eventual cash generation are subsequent tests.</p>
<p>There is a credible case that Monday was the beginning of a broader recovery. Small caps advanced, and a drop in crude can help businesses whose expenses rise with fuel. The closing returns still leave open whether non-tech stocks can carry gains on a day when chip shares pause. If small caps keep narrowing their remaining gap while semiconductor shares stall, that would provide stronger evidence of a recovery with multiple drivers than a session when both rise together.</p>
<h2>Give the Client Both Starting Dates</h2>
<p>Monday’s Morning Edition showed why the period on a Canadian-dollar statement can change how much protection investors attribute to the U.S. dollar. The afternoon adds the same discipline on the equity side. In early trading, Reuters reported Toronto’s benchmark up 2.78% as materials rose 6.2%. The Canadian Press also reported a weaker loonie in its early snapshot. Those observations show why an advisor needs to separate underlying equity performance from currency translation and from the resource exposure on the Canadian side.</p>
<p>For a household holding a U.S. broad-market fund and a separate growth or small-cap sleeve, run two comparisons using the same dates across the holdings. Measure each sleeve from Friday’s close to Monday’s close, then from November 13 through Monday. Use benchmark or fund returns in their underlying currency first and attribute the Canadian-dollar effect separately. The first window identifies what drove the bounce. The second asks how much of the original drawdown the sleeves actually absorbed together. A client who sees three funds rally at different speeds on Monday may have had almost the same percentage loss in all three over the period that mattered.</p>
